          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been diagnosed with high blood pressure.",
          "answer": "**What a hypertension diagnosis means**\n\nHypertension means blood pressure that stays raised over time, not just on one occasion. Blood pressure has two numbers. The top number (systolic) is the pressure when the heart beats. The bottom number (diastolic) is the pressure between beats. Both are measured in millimeters of mercury. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nBlood pressure changes through the day, so a diagnosis normally rests on repeated readings, not one high reading. Hypertension often causes no symptoms, which is why it is usually found during routine checks. (Same source.)\n\n**How it is classified**\n\nFor adults, US society guidance sorts office readings as follows (source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*):\n\n- **Normal:** top number below 120 and bottom number below 80.\n- **Elevated:** top number 120 to 129, bottom number below 80.\n- **Stage 1 hypertension:** top number 130 to 139, or bottom number 80 to 89.\n- **Stage 2 hypertension:** top number 140 or higher, or bottom number 90 or higher.\n\nIf the two numbers fall into different categories, the higher category applies. Guidance from other bodies uses different thresholds, so the stage that applies to you depends on which guidance your clinician follows.\n\n**Lifestyle measures**\n\nI could not retrieve the lifestyle guidance for this answer because the lookup was unavailable. I have not filled that gap from memory. Ask your clinician or pharmacist which lifestyle changes they recommend for you, and whether they want a home blood-pressure log.\n\n**Questions to raise with your clinician**\n\n- Which stage applies to my readings, and what target do you use?\n- How often should my blood pressure be checked, and should I measure it at home?\n- Which lifestyle changes are most important for me?\n- Are there any medicines or over-the-counter products I should check with you about?\n\nIf you develop chest pain, severe headache, sudden weakness, trouble speaking, or shortness of breath, seek emergency care right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
